Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text into HTML formatted text:

``````
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::sstream htmlStream;
    std::string html = "";
    htmlStream << markdown;
    htmlStream >> html;
    return html;
}
```

This function uses a simple stream approach to transform the markdown text into an HTML string. A `std::stringstream` object is used to write the markdown text into the stream, which then reads the resulting HTML text from the stream.

Here's an example usage for this function:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# World,\n\nThis is some markdown text";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Raw: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This program should output: 
```Raw: # World,\n\nThis is some markdown text\nHTML: <h1> World</h1,\n<p>This is some markdown text</p
```

This shows that the markdown text is successfully converted to HTML formatted text.

